-- Generated by scripts/pages.ts from recipes/: edit those, not this file.
-- pages[chef][spell] = { paragraph, ... }: the chef's page of each dish.
local _, ns = ...
ns.pages = {
  kendor = {
    [2538] = { "Every cook begins here, with a fire, a knife and a wolf that did not want to be dinner. Most of them never move on, which is how the roads of this kingdom came to smell of burnt wolf. Charring is not burning. You want the outside dark and crisp and the inside still yielding, and that means a fire that has settled to coals, not one still showing off its flames. Learn this, and you have learned patience. Then come and see me." },
    [2539] = { "The second dish every trainer teaches, and the first one most cooks get wrong. They believe spice is a disguise. They take a wolf that has run itself lean in the hills and bury it, and the wolf deserved better. Rub the spice in before the meat meets the fire, then leave it to settle. Spice added at the end sits on the meat like a hat. Spice added at the start becomes part of it." },
    [2540] = { "Elwynn's boars have fed this city since before it had walls, and they will feed it after the walls need rebuilding again. Tomas in Goldshire roasts them whole for his inn, turning the spit by hand and calling it tradition. It is a fine tradition. It is also why his boar is cooked on one side and raw on the other. Cut the meat into even pieces, so the heat treats them all alike. Fairness, in a kitchen, is a matter of knife work." },
    [2541] = { "The coyotes of Westfall eat whatever the farmers left behind, and the meat tastes of a hard life. You cannot change its past. You can respect it. Cut the steak thick, sear it hard, and take it off the heat while the middle still blushes. A thin coyote steak cooked through is a sandal. A thick one, rested, is supper. Every apprentice arrives thinking a steak is a simple thing. It is. That is why it is so easy to ruin." },
    [2542] = { "Goretusk liver pie is the pride of the Westfall farms, or was, when there were Westfall farms to be proud of. A farmer's wife on the coast road taught it to half the province, and I sell it here with a few corrections she has not yet travelled to Stormwind to dispute. The liver of a goretusk is strong, dark and rich, and it must be rinsed in cold water before it is cooked, to calm its bitterness. Then mild spice, and an oven that never hurries. A hot oven makes liver grey and grainy, with a taste of iron. A slow one leaves it soft and sweet and tasting of the farm it came from. The crust is a matter of opinion. The liver is not. When the pie comes out, let it rest until it stops whispering. Then cut it, and then you may be proud." },
    [2543] = { "I sell the recipe for this stew, which tells you I approve of it, the way one approves of a sturdy pair of boots. Westfall's farmers made it from whatever the land threw at them, the vulture, the snout of a goretusk, the murloc's eye, and they were right about all three. I tried it once without the eye. It was thin, and so was my pride. The eye thickens the broth. Let it simmer longer than you think it needs, then longer again. A stew rushed is a soup that lied. These days the farms stand empty and the stew is cooked by the people who lost them, in camps along the road to Sentinel Hill. It is not refined. It is honest, and in Westfall that is rarer now than refinement." },
    [2544] = { "Every trainer teaches crab cakes, and every trainer teaches them too wet. Crawler meat, picked clean, a pinch of mild spice, pressed together with your hands and nothing else, then fried until the outside sets into a crust that holds the rest. If it falls apart, you pressed too little. If it bounces, you pressed too much. A crab cake should be firm the way a handshake is firm, and no firmer." },
    [2545] = { "The Westfall coast is crawling with crabs, and the Defias smugglers who hide there have stolen my recipe for their claws. I am flattered. A claw goes to the fire cracked, or the meat steams in its own armour and comes out woolly. Push mild spice into the crack with your thumb and give it a short, hot fire. Then dig the meat out with a fork, and remember that even thieves eat well when they steal from the right man." },
    [2546] = { "Dry ribs are a farmer's dish, a boar's ribs and a rub of spice with no sauce at all. Some cooks are frightened of that. They believe a rib must drip to be good. A rib must be cooked to be good. Rub the spice in the night before, let the fire die down to a low glow, and give the ribs the whole afternoon. They come out dark, crisp and dry to the touch, and they leave nothing on the bone." },
    [2547] = { "Redridge goulash is spider and condor, stewed together by the people of Lakeshire, who have had spiders and condors to spare since the gnolls took the rest. The two meats cook at very different speeds, and the art is in the timing. The condor needs most of the afternoon to soften. The spider needs a fraction of it and goes stringy if it stays longer. Add it late. I sell the recipe with that warning written in the margin, in my own hand." },
    [2548] = { "The gnolls of Redridge carry this recipe around in their packs, stolen from some farmhouse, and do nothing with it. I sell the same recipe in Stormwind and do a great deal with it. Boar ribs and hot spice, and the whole secret is the turning. Turn them often, and the fat that renders out bastes the meat on its way down. Succulent is not a promise in the name. It is a reward for attention." },
    [2549] = { "This is the dish I would put my name to, and I have. Lean wolf flank and Stormwind Seasoning Herbs, nothing else, and anyone who offers you a substitute for the herbs is selling something. Leave a gap between the pieces on the skewer, so the fire can get at every face of the meat. Rest it before you eat it. A kabob rushed off the fire weeps on the plate, and so will you." },
    [2795] = { "Ragnar Thunderbrew believes this recipe belongs to his family. I sell it in Stormwind at a fair price, and I have improved it. He will tell you the ribs need Rhapsody Malt from the first minute to the last. They do not. The dwarves baste from the start and then wonder why the outside burns while the inside sulks. Roast the rib with nothing on it until the fat runs clear. Then, and not a minute before, brush on the malt in thin coats, each one dry before the next, so it builds a glaze instead of a char. The result is sweet, dark and sticky, and it is the finest thing to come out of Dun Morogh. I would tell Ragnar so, if he could hear anything over his own tavern. He knows. His ribs have improved since." },
    [3370] = { "Crocolisk is the steak for those who think beef too obliging. The Wetlands are full of them, and the hunters of Menethil bring me the tails in barrels of brine. For all the teeth, the tail eats like a fish, white and close-grained, and it must be cooked as a thick fish is: a fierce heat, briefly, and once. Crocolisk warmed over the next day is a punishment I reserve for pupils who left it out." },
    [3371] = { "Thelsamar's dwarves make blood sausage every winter and send a few to Ironforge, where Daryl Riknussun praises them loudly and then teaches his students to make them worse. I sell the recipe here, corrected. Bear meat, cleaned intestines and spider ichor, which binds as blood once did and keeps far better. Stuff the casing loosely. A tight sausage splits in the pan and empties itself onto the coals, and no one has ever loved a sausage that gave up." },
    [3372] = { "Murloc fins make a soup with more body than you would believe, if you have only met murlocs at the end of a sword. Simmer the fins with hot spice until they dissolve into the broth and give it a richness like the best bone stock. The murlocs would object, if anyone asked them. The murlocs object to everything, at length, and none of it in a language I care to learn." },
    [3373] = { "Gumbo is a dish of the swamps, a thick, dark stew that the people of the Wetlands cook as though their lives depended on it, which in the Wetlands they sometimes do. Tender crocolisk meat and hot spice go into the pot and cook down slowly, until the gumbo coats a spoon and stays there. The colour is the measure. Too pale, and it was hurried. Too dark, and you have burnt the bottom of the pot and should start again with dignity." },
    [3376] = { "Curiously tasty is the most honest name in my catalogue, because everyone who eats it is curious why it is so good. It is a raptor egg, which is richer than a hen's by a distance, and hot spice. A fierce whisk, a quick pan, a gentle fold. A raptor's egg is slow to set at the centre, and a cook who rushes it serves the centre raw. The curiosity is earned. The omelette is mine, by right of improvement." },
    [3377] = { "I sell a recipe called gooey spider cake, and I sell it with a straight face, because it is very good. Gooey spider legs, picked of their meat, which is mixed with hot spice, pressed into cakes and fried until there is a crust worth the name and the inside is still soft. The goo is in the name and should stay in the middle. A cake that leaks has been fried too hot. The people of Duskwood eat these by the dozen, which is the bravest thing I know about Duskwood." },
    [3397] = { "The bears of Ashenvale grow larger than ours, on a diet of night elf patience and whatever they find under the trees. Their meat is dark and rich and wants a hot spice to cut it. Sear it hard and rest it for longer than seems decent, longer than a smaller animal, because a big steak keeps cooking after it leaves the fire. In Desolace, a goblin machine sells my recipes one copy at a time. I would like to meet its cook." },
    [3398] = { "Lions of the Barrens, cut into chops and burned with hot spice by cooks who call the result a delicacy. I call it a lion that deserved better. The sweetest meat on a lion lies against the bone, so the chop is cooked on the bone, and anyone who bones it first deserves the result. Hot spice is welcome. Hot fire is not. Give the chops a moderate heat and turn them often. A lion should arrive at the table looking as if it lost a fair fight." },
    [3399] = { "Tasty, it calls itself, and it is too modest by half. Lion and soothing spice, left whole as a steak, which is the only civilised way to eat a lion. The soothing spice is not there to calm the cook. It rounds the meat's sweetness. Sear it, rest it, and cut against the grain. A lion steak sliced along the grain chews like an argument you cannot win, and I have watched pupils try." },
    [3400] = { "Bisque is a smooth soup, and turtle bisque is smooth because the turtle has given up everything it had to give. Turtle meat and soothing spice simmer through the afternoon, and then the whole pot goes through a fine sieve. Press it with the back of a ladle until nothing is left that a spoon could catch on. Skip that step and you have turtle soup, which is a different and lesser thing. Soothing, it calls itself. I would say patient, and I would be right." },
    [4094] = { "In the Badlands they will tell you a buzzard is not worth the coals. They have not eaten it barbecued. A buzzard wing is mostly sinew and spite, so it wants hot spice and hours of gentle heat, and only at the very end a fierce blaze for the skin. The hunters of Arathi sell the recipe for a pittance, which is a crime against cooking, and I have reported it to no one." },
    [6412] = { "The night elves of Teldrassil will teach you this kabob for the price of a few spider legs, which tells you what they think of spiders. Alegorn in Darnassus would have you thread each leg with a sprig of something green and arrange the skewers like a poem. Ignore the poem. Spider legs want a fierce, short heat, enough to crack the shell and no more, or the meat inside turns to string. Then you may garnish, if you must. I never must." },
    [6413] = { "A dish of the Horde, from the dust of Durotar, and the surprise is that anyone eats a scorpid on purpose. I have no need to taste the cooking of Orgrimmar. I have, however, read the recipe, because a master reads everything. Its one wisdom is the stinger, which must be cooked until it is opaque all through. Then the venom is gone and the bite remains. Undercooked, it keeps both. I leave you to decide which of those you would call a surprise." },
    [6414] = { "Kodo is the meat of Mulgore, roasted there by tauren who cook under open sky with the plains for a kitchen. I have never stood under that sky, and I have no plans to. A kodo is an enormous, patient animal, and its meat is patient too: tough at first, generous at the end. Roast it slowly and give it a long rest before the knife. The tauren are right about one thing, and it is this: some meat cannot be hurried by anyone." },
    [6415] = { "The frenzies of Darkshore are all teeth and bad manners, and the night elves fillet them with a reverence I find excessive. Alegorn would have you lay the fillet on a leaf and murmur over it. I would have you season it lightly and cook it fast, because frenzy flesh is soft and falls apart if you so much as look at it slowly. A hot pan, the skin first, and a single turn when the flesh goes white at the edge. The leaf is optional. The murmuring is not my business." },
    [6416] = { "Strider is the long-legged bird of the Barrens, all neck and opinion, and the Horde stews it with apples, which is the first sensible thing I have heard of them doing. The apple sweetens the broth and softens the meat, and the meat needs softening. Simmer it slowly until the apple has gone to nothing and the bird has given up its stubbornness. I would not cross the sea for it." },
    [6417] = { "A stew of rats, taught by the Horde in the Barrens to anyone who will catch the rats for them. I include it because I am thorough, not because I recommend it. A dig rat is small and lean and cleaner than the rats of our own sewers, which is faint praise. Stew it long. Everything about this dish suggests long cooking, including the cook's need to forget what is in the pot." },
    [6418] = { "A Barrens dish, sold at the Crossroads and eaten by the Horde with their fingers. I mention the fingers only so that you do not imitate them. A thunder lizard's tail is all muscle, and the crisp comes from cooking it in its own fat until the skin shatters. Hot spice, a hot pan, and a cook brave enough to leave it there. I will not pretend to admire the Barrens. Eat it with a knife and fork, and it is very nearly civilised." },
    [6419] = { "Venison is the meat of a cook with good manners. A stag is lean and proud and must be treated as such, with plenty of spice rubbed in and very little time on the fire. Overcook a stag and you have insulted the forest. The elves of Ashenvale sell this recipe, which I find curious, since they spend so much of their lives keeping anyone from hunting their stags. Cook it rare, slice it thin, and say nothing about where it came from." },
    [6499] = { "A clam is a mouthful of the sea, and the only way to ruin it is to forget it in the pot, which is what everyone does. The sailors at the harbour boil them in seawater and call it tradition. I call it laziness by the sea. Use good spring water, count to sixty slowly, and take them out. Rubber is for boots. If you feel the need for a sauce, you overcooked them." },
    [6500] = { "The goblins devil their clams the way they do business: with too much heat and too much noise. I teach the recipe anyway, and I correct the heat. Tangy clam meat, hot spice, and a fire you can hold your hand near for a moment without regret. A clam over a furious fire shrinks to a button, and a button chews. A clam cooked well has just enough fight left to remind you it came from the sea. The goblins would charge you double for the button." },
    [6501] = { "Chowder is clams and milk, and the milk must never boil. That is the entire difficulty, and it is enough to defeat most cooks on the coast. The clam meat goes in with a little mild spice once the milk is warm, and from then on the pot stays below a simmer until it reaches the bowl. Boiled milk splits, and split chowder looks like a mistake in a bowl. The fishwives of Westfall get it right more often than the inns. The inns should be ashamed." },
    [7213] = { "Booty Bay's cooks scorch a giant clam in hot spice and serve it to pirates, who would eat anything that did not bite back and several things that did. I have refined it. Giant clam meat is dense and sweet, and the trick is the knife: slice it so thin that the blade shows through, and the spice will reach the middle. Then a fierce fire, and not for long. A scorcho should scorch the cook's eyebrows and spare the clam." },
    [7751] = { "The first fish of every child with a pole, and the first fish many cooks never get past. A smallfish is a mouthful, and a mouthful forgives nothing. Cook it whole, in a hot pan, and only until the eye clouds over. That is your clock, and the fish carries it for you. The fishermen at the harbour sell these by the bucket to children, who burn them. I do not sell them. I correct them." },
    [7752] = { "Mackerel is an oily fish, and oil is a gift that most cooks treat as a problem. They drown it, they scrape it, they apologise for it. Put it skin first into a dry, hot pan and let its own oil do the cooking. The skin crisps, the flesh stays sweet, and the kitchen smells of the sea for a day, which is no punishment. I learned this on the docks as a boy. I have improved it only slightly, which for me is a compliment." },
    [7753] = { "A snapper from the muddy bottoms of slow rivers, and it tastes of them unless you know better. I know better. Clean it well and cook it whole, skin on, over a moderate fire, then peel the skin away before serving, and the mud goes with it. Fishermen eat it skin and all and tell you the river is part of the charm. The river belongs in the river, not on the plate." },
    [7754] = { "The dwarves of Loch Modan named this dish a delight, which is the most generous word I have ever heard a dwarf use about anything without malt in it. The frenzy of the loch is firmer than its southern cousins, cold-water flesh that holds together in the pan. Season it with mild spice, cook it quickly and serve it hot. The name may stand. The dwarves' method, which is mostly shouting, may not." },
    [7755] = { "Catfish is a river fish, wide-mouthed and whiskered and generally despised by people who have never cooked it properly. Their mistake is the skin. It holds every flavour of the riverbed, and no one has ever asked me for a riverbed. Strip it away before the pan sees the fish, and what remains is mild, sweet and firm enough to fry hot without falling apart. The fishermen of the Hillsbrad rivers swear by it, and then fry it whole, which explains their reputation as cooks." },
    [7827] = { "Albacore is a fish for people who think fish should behave like meat, and for once the fish agrees. Its flesh is firm, dark and rich, so give it a hard sear and stop long before the centre is cooked through. The sailors of Menethil cook it until it is grey from edge to edge and blame the fish. I sell no recipe for it, but I would, and I would charge more. A sailor's albacore is a ration. Mine is a meal." },
    [7828] = { "Cod is the fish of cold seas and honest kitchens, and rockscale cod is the best of them, so called because its scales are as hard as slate. Scale it before you do anything else, with a good blade and some patience. Dry it, lay it in a hot pan and turn it once, when the edge goes white. The flesh is white and comes apart in great clean flakes, and it needs nothing, not even an opinion, which is the hardest thing to withhold from a cod." },
    [8238] = { "Deviate fish live in the poisoned pools of the Wailing Caverns, in the Barrens, and I have never had the slightest wish to fetch one. The recipe is found in the bellies of the kodos that wander there, which tells you how the kodos feel about it. Cook it through, with mild spice, and eat it at your own risk. Its consequences for those who eat it are, by every account, peculiar. I am told a great many things about the Barrens." },
    [8604] = { "Every cook in this city believes he can bake an egg. Most are wrong in the same way: too hot, too long, and in a hurry to be done with it. It is the first dish I teach and the one I judge a cook by. Season the white, never the yolk. The yolk is already perfect and does not want your opinion. Tomas in Goldshire makes a perfectly respectable one. I taught him." },
    [8607] = { "Smoking is the dwarves' art, and I say so without pleasure. In Loch Modan they hang bear meat in the smoke of their chimneys and forget it there through the winter, and by spring it is better than anything they meant to cook. The secret is distance. The meat hangs high, out of the heat's reach, so that only the smoke does the work. Close to the fire, you are roasting badly. Far from it, you are smoking well." },
    [9513] = { "Thistle tea is the drink of this kingdom's rogues, who brew it from swiftthistle and spring water before doing whatever it is rogues do. I do not ask. The water should have stopped boiling a breath before it meets the leaf, and the leaf should leave the cup the moment the colour comes, green with a little gold. Left longer, it grows harsh. Rogues drink it harsh. I prefer to know where my purse is while they drink it." },
    [13028] = { "Goldthorn grows in the hard places, Stranglethorn and the Hinterlands, and it makes a tea that every trainer teaches and every soldier carries. It is strong, and it is a mistake to insult it with a furious kettle. Let the kettle rest a moment, pour, and lift the goldthorn out when the cup goes the amber of a good lamp. A goldthorn left in the cup makes the tea bitter enough to strip paint. I have seen it done. The paint did not recover." },
    [15853] = { "A plain steak from the flank of a wolf, and plainness is the hardest thing to cook. There is no sauce to hide behind and no stew to bury a mistake in. The flank is lean, so it must be seared fast and rested long, with a little spice to remind it that it is food. Rare is correct. Medium is forgivable. Beyond that, I suggest a different trade, and I know several that need no fire at all." },
    [15855] = { "Roast raptor is soldiers' food, sold at the camps in Stranglethorn where men eat what tried to eat them. That is reason enough to respect it. Raptor flesh is lean and quick, so it roasts best in large pieces that protect their own middles. Spice the outside hot, sear it over a strong fire, and move it off the heat to finish. A raptor roasted like a chicken comes out like a boot. A raptor roasted like a raptor comes out like a victory." },
    [15856] = { "The wolves of the far south are red, ill-tempered and larger than ours, and their ribs come well furnished with meat. Feralas sells this recipe to anyone passing through, which few do. Hot spice, a hot fire, and then patience, because red wolf is fattier than our grey kind and needs time to render. When the fat runs clear and the bones turn dark, they are done. When the fat smokes, so is your reputation." },
    [15861] = { "A stew from Stranglethorn, made by soldiers who had tiger meat, spring water and apples, and the wit to put them together. The apples sweeten what would otherwise be a hard, gamey broth. Brown the tiger hard first, because a stew begun pale stays pale. The water and the apples follow, and it simmers until the fruit has disappeared. A jungle produces a great deal of food and very few recipes. This is one of the good ones." },
    [15863] = { "Carrion Surprise. I have read the recipe twice, in case the name was a joke. It is not. Mystery meat and hot spice, cooked into a dish that the trolls of the Swamp of Sorrows sell as though it were a kindness. The meat must be cooked through and then cooked through again, because whatever it once was, it has been dead a while. I do not serve this. I teach it, so that my pupils know what they are avoiding." },
    [15865] = { "Mystery Stew is mystery meat stewed in dwarven stout, and the mystery is which of the two does more of the work. I will tell you: the stout. A good dwarven stout stains the meat dark, loosens it, and leaves the broth a bitterness that hides a multitude of sins. Daryl Riknussun teaches this in Ironforge as a dwarven dish, which is generous of him, since the dwarves contributed only the beer and the questions. Cook it long. Whatever the meat once was, it must forget. Skim the scum as it rises. A boil sours the stout and turns the meat to string. Served in Theramore and Desolace to people who have stopped asking questions, it is, I admit, very good. I have stopped asking questions too. It was easier than I expected." },
    [15906] = { "This dish began as a dare in some tavern no one admits to drinking in. It has outgrown its origins, as I did. Most cooks treat it as a contest and cook it as a weapon, which says more about the cooks than the chili. Mystery Meat is a name, not an excuse. Trim it as if it had a reputation to lose. Toast the spices before anything else meets them, so they wake up. The Small Flame Sac goes in last, pierced once, and you stand well back. You will understand why the day you do it wrong. Made properly, the heat arrives late and leaves slowly, like a good guest. Made badly, it arrives first and stays all evening, and the Night Watch is welcome to it." },
    [15910] = { "The kodos of Desolace grow larger than any in the Barrens while eating nothing at all, and their meat is denser than any I sell. I have no need to taste the cooking of the Horde, and I make an exception for this stew, because it is less Horde than it is kodo, and the kodo is blameless. Soothing spice and spring water, and a day's patience, on a fire so low it hardly counts as one. The meat goes in cold and comes out in threads. Do not hurry it, and do not lift the lid every hour to see how it is doing. It is doing what kodo does: taking its time. In Desolace they sell this recipe within sight of the kodo bones that litter the valley, which seems to me a little indelicate, and entirely practical." },
    [15915] = { "Chili crab is a dish of the hot south, where cooks believe spice is a form of hospitality. Tender crab meat, more hot spice than seems wise, and a pan so hot the kitchen coughs. The crab must be cooked in the spice, not under it, so stir it constantly. It is messy to eat and impossible to eat politely. I serve it with a warning and a large napkin, and I keep the napkin." },
    [15933] = { "A monster omelette is made from a giant egg, and a giant egg is enough to feed a table of six or one orc. The eggs come from the great birds of the north, sold in Felwood and Winterspring by people who have learned not to ask where they were gathered. Beat it with the soothing spice for longer than your arm likes. Then take the widest pan you own, set it over a moderate heat, and fold the set edges towards the centre with a fork. The temptation is to rush it, because it is large and the guests are watching. Resist. A monster omelette cooked fast is brown outside and raw within, and nobody forgives that. Fold it when the top is barely set and let the heat finish the middle on its way to the table. Then carry it in with both hands, and enjoy the silence." },
    [15935] = { "This recipe comes from Tirisfal, where the cooks are dead and the bats are not, yet. It reached me through a merchant I trust more than his goods. I will grant it this: a wing is mostly skin, and skin rewards a cook who leaves it alone. Pat it dry, press it flat in a hot iron pan, and hold your nerve until it lets go on its own. That is the whole art. The rest of Tirisfal's cooking I leave to Tirisfal." },
    [18238] = { "Yellowtail out of the Tanaris shallows, sold by a goblin who weighs his fish with one hand and the customer with the other. The fish is excellent anyway. It is firm and oily and needs only a hot pan and a single turn. Do not fuss with it, however much your hands want to. A spotted yellowtail is the rare fish that is better the less a cook does, and many cooks find that unbearable." },
    [18239] = { "A big, glossy fish of the southern seas, and a rich one, cut into thick steaks. Rich fish tempts a cook to fight it with strong spice. Do not. Soothing spice is the right partner, because it agrees with the fish instead of arguing. Sear the steaks dark and give them time to settle off the heat. The sailors of Booty Bay cook it in great slabs over driftwood and eat it with their knives. I have seen it, and I would rather not describe it." },
    [18240] = { "Squid has two good cooking times, very short and very long, and everything between turns it to rubber. I prefer the short. Score the body in a crosshatch, rub in a little soothing spice, and put it over the fiercest grill in the house for as long as it takes to say its name twice. It curls, it chars at the edges, it is done. The cooks of the south give it an hour in a stew. They are wrong, but in an interesting way." },
    [18241] = { "Redgill is a coastal fish, named for the flush along its gills, which fades once it is caught and cooked, as most things do. It needs nothing. A fillet, a hot pan, and the restraint to leave the skin alone until the pan lets go of it. I have watched cooks reach for every spice on the shelf with this fish, out of nerves. A good redgill is not a problem to be solved. It is a fish to be left alone." },
    [18242] = { "Hot smoking is smoking at a heat that also cooks, which is faster than cold smoking and less forgiving. Hot spice goes on the summer bass. Then the bass goes into the thick of the smoke, near enough to the coals to cook through in the time cold smoke would barely colour it. Stay with it. It is done when the flesh flakes at the thickest part, and soon after that it is dry. It is the only fish I would trust to a soldier's pack, and I have trusted it to several." },
    [18243] = { "Nightfin is a snapper caught after dark, which is how the fishermen explain their hours and their tempers. The soup is the fish and spring water, nothing else, and that is exactly enough. Simmer the snapper until the flesh slips from the bones, and take out every bone before the broth goes back on the heat. Keep the pot where a bubble breaks now and then and no oftener, and the broth stays clear. If it clouds, you boiled it. If it is clear, hold it to the light and be pleased with yourself, briefly." },
    [18244] = { "Poaching is the art of cooking in water that never quite boils, and it is the most neglected art in any kitchen. The sunscale salmon, from the warm seas, is rich and soft and would fall apart under a harsher method. Bring the water to a shiver, not a boil, lower the fish in and leave it. When it turns opaque, lift it out. The water should never once have raised its voice." },
    [18245] = { "Darkclaw lobster, from the cold shores of the north, stewed in spring water and nothing else. A lobster needs no help, only respect. Bring the water to a simmer and give the lobster to it whole. It is done when the shell goes red, and not a breath later. Shell it, and return the meat to the broth only to warm. A lobster cooked twice is an insult. A lobster warmed twice is a stew." },
    [18246] = { "The mightfish is the one fish I butcher as if it were beef, because it is cut into steaks and behaves like them. Hot spice and soothing spice, the first for vigour and the second for manners, pressed into both faces of the steak. A pan as hot as you dare, both sides seared dark, and then leave it on the board a while before the knife. The fishermen of the far south bring these in with nets and much shouting. I prefer them on a plate, in silence, as a steak ought to be eaten." },
    [18247] = { "Whitescale salmon, a cold-river fish, baked whole with soothing spice in an oven that is warm rather than hot. A whole fish bakes evenly where a fillet dries at the edges, and the skin keeps the juice where it belongs. Slide a knife along the backbone: when the flesh lifts from the bone in one piece, the fish is done. The recipe is sold in Feralas, and I would make the journey for it, if Feralas were nearer." },
    [20626] = { "A chowder from Undermine, sold in Tanaris by a goblin named Jabbey who never has more than a single copy to sell and enjoys the scarcity more than the soup. The soup is better than the goblin. Zesty clam meat and hot spice go into ice cold milk, and the pot is warmed with great care, because milk takes its revenge on a careless cook by splitting. In Undermine they serve it scalding, to show they can. I recommend eating it at a temperature that does not require a healer." },
    [20916] = { "A trout with a silvery head the colour of mithril, which explains the name, and a temper to match, which explains the fishermen's bandages. It is a mountain fish and cold-blooded in every sense. Keep the skin on, and spoon the pan's own fat over the back as it cooks. Its meat is pale and fine. The head stays on for the table. A trout without its head looks embarrassed, and so does the cook who served it." },
    [21143] = { "A Winter Veil biscuit, sold for a few weeks each year by smiling merchants in red, and made by children who eat half of it before it reaches the oven. The holiday spices are the whole of it: warm, sweet and sharp, and they must be fresh, because stale spice tastes of last year. An egg binds it. Take them out while the middles are still soft to the touch, because they go on hardening as they cool. A biscuit baked hard in the oven is a weapon by morning." },
    [21144] = { "Egg nog is the drink of Winter Veil and the reason so many Winter Veil mornings begin late. The egg is beaten into ice cold milk with the holiday spices until it is pale, thick and smells like the inside of a good cupboard. The holiday spirits come last. Measure them as if the guests could see you, which they can. Serve it cold, in small cups, and do not refill the cups of people who have begun to sing." },
    [21175] = { "White spider meat, pressed into a sausage and taught by every cooking trainer in the land as though it were ordinary. It is not ordinary. Spider meat is mild and fine-textured, closer to crab than to pork, and it takes no spice because it needs none. The art is the pressing: tight enough to hold together, loose enough to stay tender. Many pupils pale at the word spider. I ask them whether they have ever eaten a crab, and wait." },
    [22480] = { "Tender wolf meat comes from the wolves of Winterspring and the far cold places, where an animal lives slowly and keeps its fat. It is the best wolf meat in the world, and it is wasted on most cooks. Soothing spice, a brief and fierce sear, and then rest it as long as you cooked it. A cook who has ruined a hundred stringy wolves in Elwynn will finally understand, on this one, what they were reaching for." },
    [22761] = { "The recipe for this dish is carried by an imp in the ruins of Dire Maul. He parts with it only after a merry chase through halls full of very angry satyrs. I know this because a pupil of mine made the chase and returned with the recipe, a limp and an opinion of imps that I share. The tuber grows in the shadow of those ruins and tastes, cooked well, like the best root you never knew existed: earthy, sweet and faintly smoky. Peel it, cut it thick so the outside caramelises before the inside is done, and give it soothing spice and a slow oven until a fork goes in without resistance. The surprise is that anything this good comes from a place that bad. I teach the recipe gladly. I do not teach the chase, and I have advised my pupil never to repeat it." },
    [24418] = { "Heavy, it says, and it is no lie: crocolisk in quantity, with soothing spice, stewed until it is closer to a pudding than a soup. Theramore's garrison lives on it through the Dustwallow winters and swears it is the only warm thing in the marsh. They are right. The danger of a stew this dense is that the heat gathers at the base of the pot, so keep a wooden spoon moving there. Most ruined stews die down there." },
    [24801] = { "This recipe is not sold. It is taught by the Cenarion Circle's cooks in Silithus, and only to those who survived a desert that wants them dead. I have not been to Silithus. I do not need to have been to know that sandworm meat is a challenge: dense, pale and fond of sand in every crease. Wash it past the point where the water runs clear, because sand hides in what looks clean. Mince it with soothing spice and shape small dumplings, pressed hard so they keep their shape in the smoke, and set them on a rack high above a low fire. The outside takes the smoke and the middle takes the steam, and when they are done they are firm, fragrant and entirely free of sand, which is the true achievement. The Circle guards it jealously, and I respect them for it. I have guarded worse for less." },
    [25659] = { "Dirge Quikcleave runs a kitchen in Gadgetzan, in the middle of a desert, where he cooks chimaerok with goblin rocket fuel and charges for the privilege. I have every reason to despise this recipe. I cannot. Chimaerok tenderloin is the finest meat on Kalimdor, dense and sweet, from beasts so far away that only the brave or the hungry ever bring it back. Deeprock salt from the dwarves' mines, hot spice, and the fuel, which does not flavour the meat so much as flatter the fire into a heat no honest coal can reach. Use a great deal of distance and very little of the fuel. Dirge would tell you otherwise. Dirge has fewer eyebrows than I do. The chops come out charred and juicy and, I admit it, the best thing a goblin ever made." },
    [25704] = { "Smoked sagefish is a Southshore dish, and the best thing in Southshore, which is a low bar, nobly cleared. The fish lives among the sage at the edge of the Hillsbrad lakes and carries its scent in the flesh. So the smoking must be cool and slow, or the herb burns off before the fish is cooked. Dust it with mild spice and keep the fire smouldering, never flaming, until the light goes. The farmers sell it from their windows. Stormwind's cooks have the method written down. The farmers never needed to." },
    [25954] = { "The greater sagefish is the sagefish grown up, with more of everything, including the smell of the herbs along its lake. Hot spice brings it forward without burying it. Lay the fillet skin down and leave it until the skin is crisp enough to tap, then turn it for a moment, no more. Delight is a strong word for a fish. I have tasted this one, and I am not withdrawing it." },
    [470359] = { "A bisque of darkclaw lobster made with holy water from Stratholme, which I am told is drinkable, though I have read the label twice and remain unconvinced. The expedition in Silithus sells it, and I suspect they invented it to use up the holy water. It works. The water is very clean and very soft, and it lets the lobster speak for itself. Simmer, sieve, and do not ask what else that water was used for." },
    [470370] = { "A recipe sold in Silithus, by the expedition's quartermasters, to soldiers who needed food that would keep in the sand. Redgill, smoked slowly until it is dry enough to travel and still tender enough to eat. No spice, only smoke. The soldiers who brought it back to Stormwind asked me to approve of it. I did, which surprised them more than the desert had." },
    [1225758] = { "Prowler steak is tiger, which the name is too polite to say. The steaks are cut generously and rubbed with holy salts, then left alone a good while before the fire is lit. The salt does quiet work in the meantime, drawing up the juice and settling the meat. Then a hot iron, briefly, on each side. I have heard hunters argue that a tiger should be cooked angry. Hunters should hunt, and leave the stove to cooks." },
    [1225759] = { "Filet o' Flank, a name with a letter missing and a dish with nothing missing at all. The red wolf flank is rubbed with holy salts, which the clergy will tell you are blessed and the merchants will tell you are expensive. They are both right. Salt pulls the water out and tightens the meat, so it cooks quickly. A blessed steak overcooked is still overcooked. The Light will not save it, and neither will I." },
    [1225760] = { "An omelette of giant eggs seasoned with holy salts, which someone decided was a sunrise. It is the colour: a very bright yolk, a pale white, and a pinch of salt that the clergy blessed and the merchants priced accordingly. Keep the heat gentle, or the yolk dulls. A sunrise cooked fast turns to noon, and nobody writes poems about noon. I serve it to priests, who appreciate the joke, and to paladins, who do not." },
    [1225762] = { "Specklefin feast is the banquet of a cook who had a great deal of fish and one raptor's worth of ambition. Twelve spotted yellowtail, three rockscale cod and four portions of raptor, with exquisite spices, which earn their name at the counter if not always in the pan. The fish are grilled whole and the raptor roasted, and the trick, as with every feast, is that it all arrives together. The cod, being thick, start the work. The yellowtail, being quick, finish it. The raptor rests while the fish cook, which suits everyone. Lay it out on the largest board you own, the raptor in the middle like a general and the fish around it in ranks. A feast is a performance. The audience eats it. You, the cook, bow afterwards and eat whatever is left, which is usually the cod." },
    [1225763] = { "A banquet is not a dish. It is a campaign. Twelve darkclaw lobsters, four portions of turtle, three buzzard wings and a pouch of exquisite spices, which are so called because the merchant who sells them needs a word for the price. The lobsters are poached, the turtle stewed, the buzzard roasted, and all of it brought to the table at once, which is the true difficulty. Every part must finish at the same moment, so begin with the slowest, the turtle, and end with the fastest, the lobsters, lifted out the instant they turn red. I have cooked a banquet like this for the nobility of Stormwind, and I will tell you what I learned: the guests remember the lobster, and the cook remembers the timing. Nobody remembers the buzzard. Cook it well anyway. Somebody always notices what you skipped." },
    [1229737] = { "Before there is a dish, there is a fire, and before there is a fire there is a cook who knows how to build one. Dry wood, laid crosswise with room to breathe, and the flame put to the bottom of the pile, never the top. Then leave it alone. A cook who prods a young fire has not learned to wait. A cook who makes a roaring one is a show-off. The right fire for cooking is the one that has stopped trying to impress you and settled down to coals." },
    [1247741] = { "A fish I have not yet seen in Stormwind's market, though the fishermen speak of it the way they speak of every fish they have not caught. Halibut is a broad, white, generous fish, and a spined one, I am told, fights back on the line and in the kitchen. Cut it thick and cook it gently, and test it with a finger: when the flakes part, it is done, and a minute later it is chalk. When someone brings me one, I will say more, and I will say it first." },
    [1249957] = { "Peacebloom is the pale flower that grows along every road, and the Frostmane trolls of Dun Morogh brew it into this tea before they attack our dwarves. I find that reassuring, somehow. Peacebloom in spring water, brought just below a boil and left until it turns pale gold. Take it off the heat before the first bubble, because a boiled flower gives up its sweetness and keeps only its stalk. Properly made, it is the calmest cup in the kingdom. The trolls, clearly, have never had one." },
    [1249958] = { "Mageroyal is a herb with a high opinion of itself, and royal tea is mageroyal steeped in ice cold milk, which gives the milk a high opinion of itself as well. The milk is warmed gently and the herb left in it until the milk takes on a pale violet tint and the smell of a fine garden. Do not boil it. Boiled milk forms a skin, and a skin on royal tea is a disgrace, and a common one. I serve it to nobles, who enjoy the irony less than I do." },
    [1249959] = { "Liferoot, steeped in melon juice, which is an odd way to make tea and a good one. The melon juice is warmed until it just steams, and the liferoot, washed and sliced, is left in it until the juice darkens to the colour of good oak. Strain it through cloth. Leave the root in too long and the ground it grew in joins you at the table, uninvited. The murlocs of Hillsbrad carry this recipe, which I take as proof that even murlocs have afternoons." },
    [1249960] = { "A tea for after a fight, by its name, made of sweet nectar and fadeleaf. Fadeleaf is the herb that rogues chew to go unseen, which seems to me a curious thing to give the wounded. The nectar is warmed to a steam, never a boil, and the fadeleaf comes out the moment the colour turns, because after that it gives only bitterness. No merchant has yet offered me this recipe. When one does, I shall buy it, and buy the healer who wrote it a drink." },
    [1249961] = { "Sungrass, steeped in moonberry juice, which is a joke about the sun and the moon that the recipe does not trouble to explain. The juice is warmed and the sungrass steeped until the drink turns the gold of late sun. Strain it and serve it warm, never hot. It is a cheerful drink, which is rare, and I have nothing cutting to say about it, which is rarer still." },
    [1249962] = { "A tea of mountain silversage and morning glory dew. The sages of Stormwind drink it while they think, which is to say all day, and charge the cost to the Crown. The dew is warmed only until it is no longer cold, and the silversage kept in only until the dew takes a faint green. It is a very good tea. It is also an expensive way to feel wise, which is the most common kind." },
    [1249963] = { "A smoothie made with a venom sac, which the vendor assured me is safe, with the confidence of a man who had not yet drunk one. The sac is broken into spring water with mild spice and the whole of it passed through fine cloth, so the drink keeps the venom's edge and leaves the venom behind. Serve it chilled. The first mouthful is a warning and the second a pleasure, which is the right order for both. I sell nothing like it. I may yet, if the vendor survives." },
    [1249964] = { "Slimy, it calls itself, and it is the most accurate name in this chapter. Spider ichor, mild spice and ice cold milk, shaken until the ichor stops sulking at the bottom of the glass and agrees to be a drink. Serve it at once, because it does not stay agreeable. I would not have invented this. I have, however, watched a gnome drink three, which I think says more about gnomes than about the smoothie." },
    [1249965] = { "I will not try to pronounce the name of this drink. I have heard a murloc try, and it did not go well for either of us. The fin must be fresh and shaken hard in melon juice with hot spice, long enough that the juice turns faintly cloudy and takes on the sea. It is sweet, then hot, then something I can only call coastal. The murlocs drink it as an insult to the rest of us. It is, I admit, a well-made insult." },
    [1249966] = { "A smoothie with a fang in it. I read the recipe twice, then a third time with my spectacles on. Sweet nectar and hot spice, with a large fang ground fine and stirred through. The fang gives the drink a chalky body and makes it a favourite of people who fight bears. Strain it well. A drink with grit in it is a drink with a complaint in it, and I do not take complaints." },
    [1249967] = { "A drink with a flame sac in it, which the fire mages consider a recommendation. Moonberry juice and soothing spice go in the glass, and the sac is pierced over it with a pin, once, at arm's length. Shake it hard and drink it fast, before the heat makes up its mind. It is the only drink I know that warms the hands from the inside. The fire mages of the Mage Quarter order it by the jug, which tells you all you need to know about fire mages." },
    [1249968] = { "A drink named for its worst ingredient, which is a rare piece of honesty in a recipe. The wicked claw is ground to dust, because a shard in the throat is a poor way to end an evening, and stirred into morning glory dew with a heavy hand of soothing spice. The spice is a peace offering. The claw refuses it. What you drink is bitter, dark and faintly hostile. I have never served it for pleasure. I serve it to pupils who boast, and they boast less afterwards." },
    [1250142] = { "Sliders are small and many, which is how young cooks like everything. The stag is chopped fine with hot spice and pressed into rounds the size of a palm. Press them once and never again: a slider pressed twice leaves its juice in the pan, where no guest will ever taste it. I do not approve of food designed to be eaten standing. I approve of these, though I eat them seated, with a knife." },
    [1250143] = { "A pamplona is meat flattened, filled and rolled, and the soaring one is made of buzzard, which is the only thing about it that soars. The wings must be boned and beaten thin under a heavy pan before the soothing spice goes on, and then rolled tight and tied. It is fiddly work, and fiddly work is where a cook is tested. A gentle oven, then the knife. The rounds show your knife work. Make sure they show something you would sign." },
    [1250144] = { "Hachee is an old farmhouse word for a slow stew of chopped meat, and here the meat is bat. I would not have chosen bat. Bat would not have chosen me. Wings, chopped, with soothing spice, and a low heat for most of an evening, until the sinew gives up and becomes something you could serve a guest. Whoever invented it was poor and clever. I respect the clever, and I have learned, over the years, to forgive the poor." },
    [1250145] = { "Turtle stewed rather than strained, which makes it a peasant's dish and none the worse for that. Turtle and a generous hand of soothing spice, simmered until a spoon divides the meat without help. Do not sieve it. The texture is the point of a stew, and a turtle stew has a great deal of texture, some of it deliberate. Serve it thick, in bowls, with no apology for being honest." },
    [1250146] = { "Au poivre means with pepper, in some foreign kitchen, and the recipe has none. It has red wolf flank and soothing spice, and the spice carries the heat so well that nobody misses the pepper. The flank is a hard-working cut and wants a fierce pan and a quick hand. What the meat leaves in the pan matters as much as the meat: scrape it up and spoon it over before serving. Never carve it in the pan it seared in. The pan remembers the heat, and so will the meat." },
    [1250147] = { "Bruscitti. I have looked for the word in three languages and found it in none. The dish is plain enough to explain itself: big bear meat, sliced thin, stacked with soothing spice between the slices and roasted slowly until the stack becomes one piece. The trick is to slice it cold, because warm meat tears under the knife. Whoever named this dish was trying to sound foreign. The dish itself has nothing to hide, and I like it better than its name." },
    [1250148] = { "A stag steak served steaming, which is to say served at once, which is how every steak ought to be served and how almost none are. Work the soothing spice deep into the stag, then lay it on an iron hot enough that it hisses the moment the meat touches. Turn it once. Then carry it straight out. If it reaches the table without steam, it waited somewhere it should not have, and someone in your kitchen owes you an apology, and very likely a new steak." },
    [1250149] = { "The tiger in this dish was not, whatever the name suggests, caught by a thunderbolt. It was caught by a hunter and a long wait, and the swiftness belongs to the cooking. Rub the tiger with soothing spice and give it a fire so fierce it must be watched, for so short a time that turning your back is a mistake. Then put it on a warm board and leave it there. A steak cut at once bleeds onto the board and arrives dry, and nobody remembers the fire." },
    [1250150] = { "A recipe I have heard described but never seen sold, which is the most dangerous kind. The raptor is long past raging by the time it reaches the pan, so the name must mean the spice, of which there is a frightening amount. Cook the ribs low until the meat pulls from the bone, then finish them hard over high flame for the crust. Serve them only to people who asked. I do not say this of every dish." },
    [1250151] = { "Brisket is the cut of a patient cook, and bear brisket is the cut of a saint. It is the toughest part of the animal, all hard work and old muscle. Rub it with hot spice and give it the lowest fire you can keep alight and the whole of a day. Somewhere in the evening it gives up and falls apart under a fork. I cannot tell you the hour. I can tell you that the cook who checks it too often never reaches it." },
    [1250152] = { "Prehistoric, as though the raptor had been waiting since the world began to be pulled apart. Perhaps it had. This is a dish of time, not skill: raptor, heavy with soothing spice, on embers you keep checking are still alive, from morning until the evening lamps. Leave it be. When it is ready, it surrenders. Tear it with forks, never a blade. A knife makes it sliced raptor, and sliced raptor is a lesser dish." },
    [1250153] = { "Rouladen is a dwarvish word, or a dwarvish claim on a word, and the dish is rolled meat: raptor, cut thin, spread with hot spice, rolled up and tied. The thinner the slice, the sooner the heat reaches the middle, and a thick roll burns outside before its heart is cooked. Colour the rolls all over first, then turn the fire down and let them be. Snip the string off before the rolls go to the plate. String on a plate is a confession that the cook stopped paying attention." },
    [1250154] = { "Twice spiced, and for once a dish tells the truth about itself. The raptor is rubbed once before it is sliced and once after it is cooked, and the second time is not for flavour but for the crust. Raptor flesh is lean and quick to dry. Slice it across the grain, thin enough to see a candle through, and give it the fire for a breath, no more. A recipe I did not invent, and would have, given time." },
    [1250155] = { "Scrambled eggs are the first test of a cook's patience, and giant scrambled eggs are the same test in a larger pan. Giant eggs and hot spice, beaten together and stirred over a low heat, slowly, always, until they gather into soft curds. A hot pan makes them rubber. A low pan makes them silk. Most cooks cannot bear to stir for that long. Most cooks should not be allowed near a giant egg." },
    [1250156] = { "A recipe that turns up in the nests of raptors, which is either a joke or a warning. The bites are made from the egg, not the beast, and a raptor egg is a heavier, richer thing than any hen ever laid. Season it more generously than you think decent, because the yolk drinks every grain. Cook the bites small and quick. A raptor egg cooked slowly turns to rubber out of spite. I suspect the mother taught it that." },
    [1250157] = { "A breakfast omelette, with an egg and a frankly alarming quantity of mild spice. Whoever wrote this recipe woke up hungry and angry. The trick of any omelette is heat and speed: whisk the egg smooth, give it a pan already hot, and keep a fork moving through it while it sets. Fold it while the middle is still soft. The recipe was found in Redridge, in the nest of a condor, which explains the anger, if not the spice." },
    [1250158] = { "Linguine is a long flat noodle, and the recipe, to its credit, does not pretend to have one. It has zesty clam meat, cut in long strips to look the part, and turned through soothing spice. A hot pan, hardly any time, and straight to the table, because clam cooked a moment too long turns tough. On the plate the strips tangle like noodles and eat like clams. I approve of the deception." },
    [1252566] = { "Whimsyfin. Somebody named a fish that, and somebody else agreed. Gnolls and murlocs carry the recipe about Redridge in their packs, and neither is known for its kitchen, so they stole it from someone who was. The fish is a little jewel of a thing, and fragile. Mild spice, barely, and a hot pan, briefly. The name promises whimsy. The dish delivers supper, which is better and far more reliable. Eat it at once." },
    [1255241] = { "A stew made with felbloom that someone has cleansed, which I am assured makes it a herb again and no longer a threat. I take the word of the druids on such matters, and on very little else. Stag meat gives it body. The giant egg goes in last, off the heat and in a thin stream, or the broth scrambles instead of turning silky. It is cooked for those coming back from Felwood, and they deserve a kinder kitchen than the forest they left." },
    [1262978] = { "Cookie is the murloc who keeps the kitchen of the Defias Brotherhood, deep in the Deadmines. I am told he cooks with a ladle in one hand and a cleaver in the other, and does not much mind which he uses on visitors. His recipe was carried out of the mine by adventurers who took it from him, and I have studied it with a professional interest I am not proud of. Two portions of mystery meat and one of heavy kodo, slow roasted with soothing spice until the kodo yields and the mystery meat has gone dark. It is a feast, which means it is made to be shared from a common platter, which I find unhygienic and the Defias, presumably, find necessary. The dish is excellent. The cook is a murloc and a criminal. I would hire him tomorrow, if he would leave the cleaver at the door." },
    [1263067] = { "An iron oven, says the recipe, and then asks for flour, spring water and soothing spice, and no iron at all. I have read it several times. I believe the oven is the vessel and the recipe is what goes into it first. A great quantity of dough is worked with spice and baked to season the iron, as one seasons a new pan. Whatever its secret, it is a master's recipe, and I do not yet possess it, which annoys me more than I can politely say." },
    [1270632] = { "A biscuit made for the small animals that follow adventurers about, and made, I note, of nothing but flour, which tells you how often the small animals are consulted. The flour is worked with a light hand and baked until pale gold. The critters adore them. I have seen a cat eat one. The cat looked at me afterwards as though it expected more, which is the only review a pet has ever given." },
    [1270633] = { "A souffle is an egg persuaded to stand up, and the Skywall souffle, named for some high place I have not visited, stands up higher than most. The whites are beaten until they hold a peak and folded into the yolks and a little flour with a light hand, and then the oven door stays shut. That is the rule. Open the door and it falls, and it will not rise again for you or anyone. A souffle is the only dish that sulks permanently." },
    [1270635] = { "Crab season, they call the errand that teaches this, which is a grand title for picking claws on a beach. The meat is picked from the claws, bound with flour and fried a mouthful at a time. Flour is there only to keep each bite in one piece. Use a pinch, not a grain more. A bite that tastes of bread is a bite that lied to you about the beach." },
    [1283400] = { "A journeyman must learn that not all wood is equal, and star wood is the lesson. It burns with a white, clean heat that the common kind never reaches, and gets there quickly. The recipe turned up in the Wailing Caverns, among druids who had other things on their minds. Build it as you would the basic fire, but do not cook on it until the flames have died right back, because this heat punishes a hurried cook. A better fire makes a better cook only if he waits for it." },
    [1291341] = { "Thick logs are the fire of a master: a trial to light, and once lit they keep the same heat all evening, steady as a hearth. A beginner wants flame. An expert wants coals that will not change their mind halfway through a roast, and only thick logs give them. Stack them so the air can climb through the middle, and wait while they take. The recipe has not yet reached my shelves, and I have asked every trader who passes. I have seen cooks who thought they did not need it, and they were never quite experts." },
    [1294014] = { "Jam from the berries of Hyjal, which grow on the slopes of a mountain the druids call sacred and which is, above all, very cold. Berries and soothing spice, reduced over a gentle fire. To test it, chill a saucer and drop a little jam on it: if it wrinkles when pushed, it is ready. Too runny, and you have made sauce. Too stiff, and you have made a brick. Sweetpaw, they call it, after the bears who raid the bushes. The bears are right about the berries." },
    [1295805] = { "A stew of the shoreline, made from what the shoreline offers, which is crab and, to my lasting regret, murloc eyes. The eyes give body to the broth, as they do in Westfall stew, and I have learned to stop arguing with them. The tender crab meat goes in last and cooks only in the heat of the broth, which keeps it sweet. Hot spice throughout. The result is briny and rich and smells of the sea before anyone has cleaned it up." },
    [1295834] = { "A recipe from the Riverglades, sold by a woman who saw no reason to give it a grander name, and she was right. Boar meat, a good deal of it, with a pinch of mild spice, roasted on the bone until the fat crackles. That is all. I have spent my life teaching cooks to resist doing more, and here is a farmer who never needed teaching. Rest it before you carve it. Plain food forgives nothing, which is why it is so seldom done well." },
    [1319313] = { "A fish that wears armour, which seems only fair, given what happens to the ones that do not. Work a heavy knife under the plates from the tail end, where they overlap, and they lift one by one. The flesh beneath is as firm as a lobster's tail and nearly as sweet. Season it with soothing spice and cook it over a moderate heat, with the patience the plates have already taught you. A fish that took so long to grow its armour may take a while to leave it." },
  },
  eunice = {
    [2538] = { "The first dish of every cook, alive or not. You learn it on the road with a knife and a fire, because there is always a wolf and there is always someone hungry. Listen to it. Wolf meat hisses when it first meets the heat and falls quiet when the outside has sealed. Turn it once, at the quiet. My students want to poke it. I let them, once, and then I let them eat what they poked." },
    [2539] = { "I teach this on the second day, after the charred wolf, so that my students learn spice is not a correction. Rub it into the meat before the fire. When it goes on at the end, it smells of spice and of wolf, two strangers at the same table not speaking. When it goes on at the start, it smells of supper. The living tell me the difference is just as plain on the tongue." },
    [2540] = { "In Lordaeron we roasted boar at harvest, in a pit behind the barn, or behind the inn, or behind somebody's barn. I remember the pit clearly and the barn not at all. Down here it is a plain dish for plain days. Roast the meat in pieces, never whole: a whole boar takes the day, and the people waiting for it will not. When the fat begins to smell sweet instead of sharp, it is ready." },
    [2541] = { "Coyotes eat whatever the world drops, and their meat remembers every meal. It takes no spice, only a hot pan and a cook who does not apologise for it. Sear it until the edges curl and the smell turns from blood to toasted nuts, then take it off the heat and leave it alone. A coyote steak is never tender, the living tell me, chewing. It is honest, and honest food keeps a traveller on their feet for a long road." },
    [2542] = { "A farm pie from the south, and we made one very like it in Lordaeron, with pig's liver, on the days the butcher had been. I can see the pie dish. It was blue, with a chip on the rim, and it sat on the windowsill to cool, and I do not know whose window it was. The goretusk's liver is strong and dark. Rinse it in cold water before you cook it, to calm the bitterness, then season it with mild spice and give it a slow oven. Liver cooked fast smells of iron and turns grey. Liver cooked slowly smells of something rich and gentle, like warm earth after rain, and that is when it is ready. My students make it for the living who pass through, and the living eat it as if it were familiar. Perhaps it is, to them." },
    [2543] = { "A farmer's stew from the south, made from what the land gives when it gives very little: vulture, a goretusk's snout and a murloc's eye, which I will not apologise for. We had a stew like it in Lordaeron, made from whatever the fields and the river spared. I remember the pot. I remember it was black and heavy, and that someone always burned their hand on it. Whose hand, I could not tell you any more. The eye gives the broth its body, so do not leave it out to spare a squeamish guest. Squeamish guests can go hungry. Let it simmer until the vulture gives up, which is longer than you think. Travellers from the south tell me the farms there stand empty now. The stew outlived the farms, as stews tend to." },
    [2544] = { "Southshore made crab cakes on holidays, and the women argued about the spice through an open window, though I no longer know whose. I remember the arguing better than the cakes. Pick the crawler meat clean, add a pinch of mild spice, and press it together with your hands. Fry it until the outside sets. If it falls apart, press harder next time. If it bounces, press less. The arguing is optional." },
    [2545] = { "Crack the claw before it meets the fire, so the heat reaches the meat inside rather than steaming it in its own shell. A pinch of mild spice into the crack. A short, hot fire. You will hear the meat start to sizzle inside the shell, a small, busy sound, and when it stops, the claw is done. Pick it out with a fork. The shell goes to the apothecaries, who want everything." },
    [2546] = { "No sauce, only spice and a low fire and the whole of an afternoon, the way the farms cooked them at harvest, though I have lost which end of it. The ribs are done when the bones show a finger's width at the ends and the smell has gone from meat to something closer to toasted bread. I serve them on paper, to people who eat with their hands and do not mind who sees. In the Undercity, that is most people." },
    [2547] = { "Spider and condor, stewed together. The condor is tough and the spider is not, so the condor goes in first and the spider much later. Put them in together and you will smell the spider overcook, a sharp, sour note, long before the condor is anywhere near done. It is a dish of the south, from people with more spiders than they wanted. I understand those people very well." },
    [2548] = { "Ribs and hot spice, turned every few minutes so the fat bastes the meat as it melts. The turning is the recipe. A rib left to itself burns on the bottom and sulks on top. A rib turned often comes out glossy and generous. A farmer taught me that, a neighbour, I think, with a red beard, or a red barn. I give this one to the students who think cooking is waiting. It is not. It is paying attention while you wait." },
    [2549] = { "Stormwind Seasoning Herbs, the packet says, and they smell exactly like a market I remember. Whether the market stood in Stormwind or in Lordaeron, I no longer know. The wolf does not care. Thread the meat with gaps between the pieces, so the heat reaches every side, and listen for the fat to spit. When it stops spitting and starts to sing, turn the skewers. The first one goes to whoever has been waiting longest." },
    [2795] = { "A dwarven dish, which means it comes with a story about whose grandfather invented it, and the story changes with whoever is telling it. I take no sides. The ribs and the malt are honest whoever cooked them first. Every dwarf I have heard of bastes from the first minute and then blames the fire for what follows. Roast the ribs bare until the fat runs clear, and only then begin the malt, a little at a time, so it turns to a glaze. When it smells of burnt sugar, you have gone too far. When it smells of bread and smoke, you are exactly right. The deathguards ask for this one by name, which is more than they do for their sergeants. I make it on feast days, and on the days that need one." },
    [3370] = { "Crocolisk reaches the Undercity from the Wetlands by way of people who would rather not say how. I do not ask. The meat is pale and firm, more fish than beast, and it wants a pinch of spice and very little time. Cook it once. Crocolisk heated a second time smells of the swamp, and the swamp is not what anyone ordered. Serve it at once, to whoever is standing nearest." },
    [3371] = { "Every autumn the farmhouses of Lordaeron made blood sausage, and every autumn someone's grandmother knew the only proper way. Mine swore by thyme. Or sage. I was certain for years. Down here the Spider Ichor binds it where blood once did, and the Royal Apothecary Society assures me it is safe for the living, which is more than they say about most things. The living come back for seconds, the only review I trust. Poach the links before they see the pan. A sausage that splits was packed by someone in a hurry." },
    [3372] = { "Murloc fins and hot spice, simmered until they melt into the broth and leave it thick and rich, like bone stock. The smell is the sea at low tide, which sounds unpleasant and is not, once the spice gets to it. The murlocs along the coast of Silverpine would object, if they knew. They object to most things, loudly, and I have long since stopped listening." },
    [3373] = { "Tender crocolisk meat and hot spice, cooked down until the broth is thick and dark enough to coat a spoon. The colour tells you, and so does the smell: when it goes from swamp to toasted nut, you are there. Stir from the bottom, always. I cannot taste a gumbo, but I can smell a burnt one from the other end of the Undercity, and so, I am told, can everyone else." },
    [3376] = { "A raptor egg and hot spice. The egg is richer than a hen's and sets more slowly, so be patient with the middle. Beat it hard, cook it fast, fold it soft. The curious part, the living say, is how good it is. I believe them, because they go quiet and then look at the plate as if it owed them an explanation. I make it for my students on their first good day." },
    [3377] = { "Spider legs, the meat drawn out and pressed into cakes with hot spice, fried until the outside crisps and the middle stays soft. The goo is meant to stay in the middle. If it leaks into the pan you will hear it hiss and smell it scorch, and the cake is lost. Fry them at a steady heat, not a fierce one. I make them by the tray. Spiders are the one thing the Undercity can always offer a guest." },
    [3397] = { "The bears of the south are bigger than ours, and their meat is darker, with a smell of pine and wet earth that the hot spice cuts through. Sear the steak until it smells of smoke and iron, then let it rest a long while. A big steak goes on cooking after the fire, and many a cook has ruined one by staring at it. I tell my students to walk away and count to a hundred, and to tell me afterwards how it tastes. Most reach forty." },
    [3398] = { "The lions of the Barrens are lean and sweet close to the bone, so the chops stay on the bone. Hot spice, a moderate fire, and many turns. The heat is for others to judge, and they do. The orcs from the Crossroads ask for more spice every time and the trolls ask for less, so I make two pans and let them argue about who has the right one." },
    [3399] = { "Lion cooked as a steak rather than chopped, with soothing spice to round the sweetness of the meat. The soothing spice smells of cloves and warm stone. Rub it in, sear the steak hard, rest it as long as you seared it. Slice it across the grain, or it fights back in the mouth. The name was written by someone who had eaten it and was too busy to find a better word." },
    [3400] = { "Turtle and soothing spice, simmered for hours, then worked through a sieve until it is smooth as cream. The sieve is the recipe. The smell while it simmers is warm and round, like cloves in a closed room, and it slows people down when they pass my kitchen. I like a dish that slows people down. Down here, everyone is in such a hurry to be somewhere they have already been." },
    [4094] = { "There are more buzzards than cooks in the Barrens, and the cooks have found a use for them. The wing wants hot spice, a long low heat to soften the sinew, then a short fierce flame to blister the skin. You can hear the change. The fat stops whispering and starts to crackle. Serve them hot and in quantity. Nobody has ever eaten only one buzzard wing." },
    [6412] = { "An elven recipe, from a tree so tall the night elves are said to cook in its branches. I have never been, and I am not expected. The recipe came down to me through three traders, two of them honest. Thread the spider legs on a skewer and give them a quick, fierce heat until the shells crack like twigs in a fire. That sound is the signal. After it, every moment costs you." },
    [6413] = { "Orgrimmar's cooks sent this north on the first zeppelins, and the Undercity took to it. The surprise is in the stinger. Cooked until it turns from glass to chalk, it loses its venom and keeps a pleasant crunch. Undercooked, it keeps the venom, and the surprise is a different one. You can hear the change. A raw stinger rattles in the pan. A done one lies still." },
    [6414] = { "The tauren of Mulgore roast kodo the way they do most things, slowly and without fuss. I cook it the same way. Kodo is a great, slow animal, and its meat is tough until it decides otherwise. A pinch of spice, a low fire, and an afternoon. It is done when it smells of warm grass and smoke. I cannot taste it, so I asked a tauren trader, who told me mine was nearly as good as his mother's. I chose to believe the nearly was politeness." },
    [6415] = { "Frenzies are all teeth and temper, and their flesh is the softest thing about them. A fillet falls apart if you look at it too long. Season it lightly, lay it skin down in a hot pan, turn it once. The smell is sweet and short-lived, gone in a breath. When it fades, the fish is done. The recipe comes from Darkshore, by way of an elf who wanted nothing from me but a fair price, which is rarer than it should be." },
    [6416] = { "Strider and apple, the first stew of the Barrens. The apple goes in with the meat and cooks away to nothing, and when you can no longer find it in the pot, the stew is done. Strider is a stubborn bird. The apple softens it. I make this for orcs who miss home and for trolls who have never had a home to miss, and they all eat it the same way, very fast." },
    [6417] = { "The Barrens taught me this, and the Undercity took to it, because we are not short of rats either, though ours are not fit to eat and the dig rats of the Barrens are. Stew them long, until the meat falls from the small bones, and lift the bones out with patience and a spoon. My students make faces. I make them eat it. Then they make it themselves, for the next students, and the faces continue." },
    [6418] = { "The Barrens taught me this one, along with patience and a respect for anything that survives there. A thunder lizard's tail is solid muscle wrapped in a skin like old leather. Cook it in its own fat with hot spice until the skin crackles under a spoon. If it bends, it is not done. If it shatters, it is perfect. The orcs eat it whole. I cut it into rounds, for the sake of the furniture." },
    [6419] = { "There were deer in the woods above the farm, and my father, or my uncle, or a neighbour with a bow, brought one down each autumn. The face is gone. The smell of the meat in the pan is not. Venison is lean and proud and wants plenty of spice and very little fire. Take it off while the middle is still dark. Lean meat cooked through smells of nothing but regret." },
    [6499] = { "Clams are honest. They tell you when they are done, if you keep your nose over the pot. The steam smells sweet at first, then of the sea, and that is the moment to lift them. Clams forgive nothing, and I respect that in a food. A traveller off the zeppelin once ate two bowls standing at my counter and asked whether there was a third. There was not. I make more now." },
    [6500] = { "Goblins put too much heat in everything, including their clams. I teach it with the heat corrected. Tangy clam meat, hot spice, a fire that is hot but not furious. The smell will tell you when to stop: when the spice stops smelling raw and starts smelling toasted, take the pan away. A clam cooked furiously turns to leather. The goblins would sell you the leather and call it chewy." },
    [6501] = { "Somebody in Southshore sold chowder from a door near the harbour, and the smell carried along the quay. I remember the smell exactly and the door not at all. Warm the milk gently, add the clam meat and a pinch of mild spice, and keep it below a simmer. Milk that boils splits, and you can smell it happen, a sour edge on the steam. If you smell it, start again." },
    [7213] = { "A giant clam is dense and sweet, and in Booty Bay they scorch it in hot spice for pirates. Slice it thin before the heat touches it, so the spice reaches through, then fire it fiercely and quickly. The smoke will make your eyes run. That is how you know you have used enough spice. When the smoke clears, the dish is done. Serve it to someone with a strong stomach and a short memory." },
    [7751] = { "Children catch these off every dock in Tirisfal, and I buy their buckets, because a child with a fish deserves a coin. Cook them whole in a hot pan, a handful at a time. Their eyes cloud when they are done, which is the kindest kind of signal: the fish tells you itself. My students who can do nothing else can do this, and I start them here so they have something to be proud of." },
    [7752] = { "Mackerel is oily, and the oil is the point. Lay it skin down on a hot, dry pan and let its own fat cook it. The smell fills the kitchen, climbs the stairs and reaches the street, and someone always comes down to ask what it is. That is how I find most of my customers. The fish advertises itself better than I could, and it asks for no wages." },
    [7753] = { "The mud is in the skin, not the flesh, and you can smell it the moment the fish meets the heat. Cook it whole over a moderate fire and lift the skin away before you serve it. The flesh beneath smells of nothing but fish, which is exactly what you want from a fish. The fishermen of Tirisfal eat the skin too. They say it reminds them of the river. They did not need to tell me why." },
    [7754] = { "The cold water of the loch makes a firmer fish than the southern frenzies, and the dwarves named it a delight, which I am told is unusual for them. A pinch of mild spice, a quick fire, and serve it hot. The smell is clean and cold, like a morning by a lake. I have never been to Loch Modan. I have been to lakes. I imagine it is similar, with more shouting." },
    [7755] = { "Catfish were what the river gave us when it gave us anything, and someone in my family skinned them on the back step. Skin it always. The skin smells of the riverbed, and the riverbed is not supper. The flesh beneath is white and sweet and wants a hot pan and one turn. I can still hear that knife on the step. I cannot see the hand that held it." },
    [7827] = { "Firm and dark and rich, more like meat than fish, and it should be cooked like meat: a hard sear outside and very little in the middle. The colour tells you. Pink inside is right, grey is too far. I cannot taste the difference. I can see it, and so can the face of whoever eats it. Pink earns a nod. Grey earns politeness, which is worse." },
    [7828] = { "Its scales would blunt a cheap knife, so buy a good one before you buy the fish. Scale it outside, if you can. The scales go everywhere and are found for weeks. Then a hot pan and a single turn. The flesh comes apart in clean white flakes and smells of cold water and salt. It needs nothing. I give it nothing, and my students learn that nothing is sometimes the hardest thing to give." },
    [8238] = { "Deviate fish come from the poisoned pools of the Wailing Caverns, and whoever poisoned them did not mean to make them delicious. They are, a little. Cook the fish through, with mild spice, and do not undercook it. The apothecaries are very interested in what it does to the living. I have watched. I will say only that it is the one dish my customers have ever eaten twice, to see what would happen." },
    [8604] = { "An egg is the first thing I teach, because it tells you everything by its smell before it tells you anything by its taste. When the kitchen smells of warm spice and nothing else, it is done. When it smells of anything sharper, start again rather than argue with it. The egg is always right. My students think an egg is beneath them. An egg has embarrassed more of them than any boar." },
    [8607] = { "No spice and no sauce, only smoke and time. This is food for people who are going somewhere. Cut the meat along the grain and hang it high over a low fire of green wood, where the heat never reaches and the smoke does all the work. In the morning the strips should bend before they break. I pack them for the deathguards on the road to the Sepulcher. They do not need to eat, and they take three each." },
    [9513] = { "Swiftthistle steeped in spring water until it turns green-gold, then strained. Every rogue in the Undercity drinks it before work, and the Undercity has a great many rogues. They come to my counter at strange hours and pay in exact coins. Do not steep it too long. It turns bitter, and the rogues, who like it bitter, will drink it anyway and leave without a word." },
    [13028] = { "A little goldthorn goes a long way, which tells you how strong it is. Steep it in water just off the boil until it turns the colour of old brass, then strain it hard. The smell is sharp and bright, like a sword being polished. Every deathguard on the walls drinks this, and none of them need to. They cannot taste it any better than I can. I think they like the warmth of the cup in their hands. I have never asked." },
    [15853] = { "A plain steak from a wolf's flank, with a pinch of spice and nothing to hide behind. That is what makes it a test. Sear it fast, rest it long, slice it thin across the grain. I judge it by its colour and by the face of whoever eats it, since I cannot do otherwise. I make my students cook it until they stop flinching at the hiss of the pan. It takes some of them a week. One took a year." },
    [15855] = { "The camps of Stranglethorn roast raptor in large pieces, and so do I, because raptor is lean and a large piece keeps its own middle moist. Hot spice on the outside, a strong fire, then pull it away from the heat to finish. The smell turns from metal to smoke to roasted nuts. Somewhere around the nuts, take it off. The raptor fought hard. Do not let it win at the table." },
    [15856] = { "Red wolves from the far south, fattier than the grey ones of Silverpine, with ribs that need time to render. Hot spice and a hot fire, then patience. The fat runs clear before it runs smoky, and the moment between is the one you want. The deathguards who come back from the south bring these up with them wrapped in cloth, as if they had found treasure. For a cook, they had." },
    [15861] = { "Tiger meat, spring water and apples, the stew of Stranglethorn. Brown the meat first, then add the water and the apples and let it simmer until the fruit has gone into the broth. The smell turns sweet, then deep, then sweet again. The second sweetness means it is ready. Trolls taught it to me, Darkspears on their way somewhere warmer. They said it tastes of home. I took their word for it." },
    [15863] = { "The trolls of the swamp call this a pie and mean it kindly. Mystery meat and hot spice, cooked until there is no doubt left about anything. I can smell what the meat was, which is more than the trolls can, and I still serve it. Cook it through, then cook it again. Whatever it was, it has had a long journey, and it deserves a firm ending." },
    [15865] = { "Mystery meat stewed in dwarven stout, and to me there is no mystery about either. I can smell exactly what the meat was. I can smell the brewery the stout came from, and what was spilled on its floor. Cook it long and gently, and never let it boil, or the stout turns sour and you will smell that from the stairs. Skim the pot as it simmers. The stout makes the meat dark and soft and gives the broth a bitterness the living seem to like a great deal. The apothecaries, who should know better, eat it in quantity and refuse to say what they think is in it. I have watched a whole table of them argue over it for an evening. I have stopped telling them. They enjoy it more not knowing, and so, I think, does everyone." },
    [15906] = { "Mystery Meat is no mystery to me. I can smell exactly what it was, and in most cases I would rather not say. The Barrens taught me this dish, not Lordaeron, which never ate anything that fought back this hard. I cannot tell you how hot this chili is. I can tell you that the toasted spices sting my eyes, which is the one part of it I still feel. The last tauren who tried mine set his spoon down very carefully and asked for water in a voice I had not heard from him before. Pierce the flame sac before it goes in, or it will pierce itself. Make it for people you like, and warn them. Then watch their faces. The dish is done when they stop talking." },
    [15910] = { "The kodos of Desolace are heavier than those of the Barrens, on a diet of very little, and their meat is the densest I know. The tauren taught me this stew, and I have never changed a thing, because there is nothing to change. Heavy kodo meat, soothing spice and spring water, and a whole day over a low fire. You will smell it turn: raw, then grassy, then deep and brown, like a field after rain. Somewhere in the evening the meat comes apart in threads at the touch of a spoon. That is the moment, and no other. I make it on the coldest days for the deathguards on the walls above, and I carry it up myself. They do not need it. They line up anyway, and they are very quiet while they eat." },
    [15915] = { "Tender crab meat and plenty of hot spice, stirred constantly in a pan hot enough to make you step back. The spice must cook into the crab, not sit on it. How hot it is, I leave to the trolls, who eat it without blinking, and to the orcs, who eat it while pretending not to blink. I serve both with the same straight face. Eat it with your hands. Nobody in my kitchen will judge you." },
    [15933] = { "A giant egg feeds a table, and in the Undercity a table is whoever is standing nearest the stove. The eggs come from the great birds of the north, from Felwood and Winterspring, carried down by traders who have learned not to say where they found them. Beat the egg with soothing spice until it is smooth and pale, then pour it into the widest pan you own over a moderate heat and draw the edges in as they set. The smell is the guide: warm and round at first, then nutty at the edges. Nutty is the moment to fold. Brown is too late. Carry it to the table with both hands. I have watched a whole patrol of deathguards divide one of these in silence, with great care, as if it were treasure. For a moment, it was. Then they asked for another, which was the best part." },
    [15935] = { "Every new cook in Tirisfal makes this, because the bats are everywhere and little else is. I have made more of them than any dish of my first life. Score the skin of the wing and lay it on a hot griddle, skin down, and leave it alone. It talks while it cooks. When the hiss turns to a crackle, turn it once and never again. Serve it to whoever is still hungry. In the Undercity, there is always someone." },
    [18238] = { "Firm and oily, from the warm waters off Tanaris, and better the less you do. Lay it in a hot pan and leave it be until the edge turns white, then turn it. The smell when it is done is clean and bright, like a sea breeze that has not yet met the docks. My students want to add things. I take the spice jar away and give it back when the fish is eaten. Most of them never ask for it again." },
    [18239] = { "A big, rich fish from the warm south, cut into steaks thick enough to rest like meat. Soothing spice rounds the richness without hiding it. Sear the steaks hard, rest them, serve them. The smell is so rich it is almost sweet, and it carries. When I cook mightfish, the queue at my counter starts before the fish is done. It never starts that early for anything I am proud of." },
    [18240] = { "A moment or an hour, nothing in between. I choose the moment. Score the squid in a crosshatch, rub a little soothing spice into the cuts and lay it on the hottest grill you have. It curls as if surprised. When the edges char, it is done. The smell is sweet and smoky, and it pulls customers in from the street like a rope." },
    [18241] = { "Redgill needs nothing but a hot pan. Lay the fillet skin side down and wait until it lifts away on its own. That is the whole lesson, and it takes most students a month to learn, because waiting is harder than doing. The smell goes from raw to sweet, and the edges go from glass to white. Lift it then. It has told you everything it knows." },
    [18242] = { "Smoked hot and close, so the fish cooks as it smokes. Rub the summer bass with hot spice and hang it just above a smoky fire. Watch the flesh: when it turns from glass to white, take it down. Bass smoked this way keeps for days. I pack it for travellers taking the zeppelin, who are always hungry and always late, and who eat it before the zeppelin leaves the tower." },
    [18243] = { "A snapper caught at night, and spring water, and nothing else. Simmer the fish gently until the flesh falls from the bones, then lift the bones out with care. The broth should be clear enough to read through. It smells of very little, which is the point: a clean, quiet soup for people who have had a loud day. I make it for myself at the end of the evening, and I do not mind that I cannot taste it." },
    [18244] = { "Water that never quite boils, for a fish that would fall apart under anything harsher. Bring it to a shiver, lower the salmon in, and leave it alone. You will see it turn from bright to pale, as if it were blushing in reverse. Lift it out then. Poaching is the gentlest thing I do in a kitchen, and the one my students find hardest, because nothing happens for so long." },
    [18245] = { "Darkclaw lobster in spring water, and that is all. Lower the lobster in whole and lift it out the moment the shell turns red. Take the meat from the shell and warm it in the broth. Never boil it twice. The smell of a lobster going too far is sharp and unmistakable, like hot copper, and once you have smelled it you will never let it happen again. I let it happen once. That was enough." },
    [18246] = { "A mightfish is big enough to cut into steaks like beef, and it should be cooked like beef. Hot spice to wake it, soothing spice to calm it, a hard sear on both sides and a long rest. The smell is of the sea and of a smithy at once. I serve it to the deathguards on the nights before a long patrol. They say nothing, which is what deathguards say when something is good." },
    [18247] = { "A whole salmon from the cold rivers, baked with soothing spice in a moderate oven. Whole, because the skin keeps the flesh moist, like a lid on a pot. When the eye turns white and the fin pulls away at a touch, it is done. The kitchen smells of rivers and warm spice, and the apothecaries come down to borrow things they do not need, and leave with a plate." },
    [20626] = { "A goblin chowder from Tanaris: zesty clam meat, hot spice and ice cold milk, warmed together without ever boiling. The goblins eat it scalding, which tells you everything about goblins. Keep the heat low and watch the steam. When it smells of the sea and of warm spice, and not yet of scorched milk, ladle it out. The goblin who sells it keeps one copy at a time. I bought it, so now there are two copies." },
    [20916] = { "A cold mountain fish with a silver head and a fighter's temper. Cook it whole, skin on, in a hot pan, and baste it with its own fat. When the skin blisters and the eye turns white, it is done. Leave the head on. My students ask why. I tell them the fish worked hard to keep it, and the least we can do is let it come to the table." },
    [21143] = { "Winter Veil in Lordaeron smelled of these, every kitchen on the street at once, or I believe it did. I remember the smell so clearly that I sometimes catch it in the Undercity in summer, which cannot be true. Holiday spices and an egg, worked together and baked until the edges darken and the middles still give. They harden as they cool. I bake them every Winter Veil for the children of the zeppelin crews. They do not ask why I bake so many." },
    [21144] = { "Winter Veil, and somebody's uncle making egg nog in a great bowl, too strong, every year. I cannot see his face, but I can hear him singing. An egg, ice cold milk, holiday spirits and holiday spices, beaten until pale and thick. The spirits go in last and with restraint, which no uncle has ever shown. It smells of nutmeg and warm wood and a little of trouble. Serve it cold and keep an eye on the bowl." },
    [21175] = { "Spiders are the one thing the Undercity is never short of. White spider meat is sweet and delicate and takes no spice at all, which is a mercy in a city that smells of everything. Press it tight with your hands so it holds together, and fry it until the outside crisps. In the pan, listen for the crust to snap under the spoon. It is the most satisfying sound in my kitchen, and my students learn to wait for it." },
    [22480] = { "The wolves of the cold places live slowly and keep their fat, and their meat is the best of any wolf. Soothing spice, a short hot sear, a long rest. You will smell when it is ready: the spice warms and the meat turns sweet, like a loaf just out of the oven. My students swear they taste the difference from the first bite, and I believe them, because they stop talking. A cook who has fought a hundred stringy wolves deserves this one. I serve it to my best students." },
    [22761] = { "The recipe comes from an imp in the ruins of Dire Maul who leads anyone who wants it on a long and stupid chase. One of my students made the chase and came back with the recipe, two new scars and a great deal to say about imps. The runn tum tuber grows in the shadows of those ruins, and it is the finest root I know, earthy and sweet. Peel it, slice it thick and roast it slowly with soothing spice until the edges darken and the middle gives to a fork. You will smell when it is right: like chestnuts on a street corner in winter. That is not my memory, it is my student's, who described it so well that I have borrowed it. The deathguards like it because it fills them. The living like it because it tastes, they tell me, of somewhere safe. I make a great deal of it." },
    [24418] = { "Crocolisk by the potful, soothing spice, and a stew that a spoon can stand in. Stir it from the bottom, often, because a stew this thick sticks and burns where you cannot see, and the smell gives you away only when it is too late. The soldiers of the marshes eat it out of tin bowls in the rain, I am told. My deathguards eat it out of tin bowls in the dry. Tin bowls are the same everywhere." },
    [24801] = { "The Cenarion Circle teaches this in Silithus to those who have proven themselves in the desert. The Horde's soldiers came back with it among their scars. Sandworm meat is dense and pale and full of sand. Wash it until the water runs clear, chop it fine with soothing spice, and roll it into small dumplings, pressed tight so they hold together in the smoke. Then smoke them slowly over a low fire. The smoke flavours the outside while the steam cooks the middle, and the smell moves from raw to sweet to something like toasted grain. When it reaches the grain, they are done. I cannot tell you how they taste. I can tell you that a soldier who had lost three friends in that desert ate a plate of them in my kitchen and wept. Then he asked for the recipe, and I gave it to him." },
    [25659] = { "A goblin in Gadgetzan cooks chimaerok with rocket fuel, and the Horde's travellers carry the recipe home like a dare. Mine came from a tauren who had lost his eyebrows and kept his appetite. Chimaerok tenderloin is a long way to go for a piece of meat, the islands off Feralas, and it is worth every step. Deeprock salt, hot spice, and the goblin rocket fuel, which goes on the fire, never in the food. Stand back further than seems polite. The flare lasts a breath and leaves a crust no honest coal could make. The smell fills the whole Apothecarium, and the apothecaries come out of their rooms to complain and stay to eat. I do not ask what they think of it. I count the plates." },
    [25704] = { "Sagefish come from the lakes of Hillsbrad, where my family went in summer, I think. The fish smells of the sage along the banks, and the smoke brings it forward. I am sure of the smell. I am less sure of the summers. Low smoke, a pinch of mild spice and an afternoon. When the flesh turns golden and smells of herbs and woodsmoke, it is done. I smoke it in batches, because the living buy it by the armful." },
    [25954] = { "The greater sagefish, grown fat on the sage along its lake, with hot spice to wake it. Sear the fillet skin side down until the skin crackles, then turn it for a breath. The smell is the delight: green and warm and peppery, all at once. I make this one when I want the kitchen to smell of something other than the city above it, which is most days, if I am honest." },
    [470359] = { "Holy water from Stratholme, which the apothecaries handed me with a straight face. I went to Stratholme once, alive, for a market or a wedding, and I remember the bells and the bread and nothing else of it. Now its water makes a bisque. Darkclaw lobster, simmered in it until the shell turns red, then sieved smooth. The water is very soft and very clean, and it does not burn me. It lets the lobster speak. I listen." },
    [470370] = { "The expedition in Silithus sells this to soldiers who need food that keeps in the sand. Redgill, smoked slowly until it is dry enough to carry and still tender enough to eat. No spice. Smoke and time do the work. I learned it from an apothecary who came back from the desert with this recipe and very little else. He said it was the only thing there that did not try to kill him." },
    [1225758] = { "Tiger, the name means, cut thick and rubbed with holy salts an hour before cooking. Tiger is a hard-working meat and the salt calms it. Then a hot iron, a short sear and a long rest. You will hear it relax on the board, a soft settling, like a dog lying down. That is when you slice. Not before. A tiger that has not settled will not settle at the table either." },
    [1225759] = { "Holy salts, from the churches of the living, which made me laugh the first time I held a pouch of them. They are good salts. The Light has nothing against me in a kitchen. Rub them into the red wolf flank and leave it an hour, then cook it quickly. The salt draws the water from the meat and firms it. It does not burn me, before you ask. My students always ask." },
    [1225760] = { "Giant eggs and holy salts, which I was warned would sting me and which do not. Cook it slowly, so the yolk stays bright as morning. The smell is clean and simple, egg and salt and the pan. I cook it at the hour the living call dawn, which down here means nothing, out of a habit I do not remember forming. Someone always comes in for it. I serve it on a white plate, so the yolk can show off." },
    [1225762] = { "Twelve spotted yellowtail, three rockscale cod, four portions of raptor and exquisite spices, laid out on the largest board in the kitchen. The cod go first, because they are thick. The raptor roasts and rests while the fish cook. The yellowtail go last, because they are quick, and the moment their skin lifts from the grill they are done. The smell of a fish feast is a tide coming in: first the sea, then the smoke, then the spice. When the spice arrives, start carrying. I made it for the zeppelin crews at the end of a long season, and the goblins, who are not sentimental, sat down at my counter and ate for an hour without once mentioning money. That is the only time I have seen a goblin forget to haggle. It was the feast, not me." },
    [1225763] = { "A banquet is a great deal of work, and this one is more than most. It asks for twelve darkclaw lobsters, four portions of turtle, three buzzard wings and exquisite spices, which are exquisite mostly in price. The turtle stews longest, so it begins first. The buzzard roasts while the turtle stews. The lobsters go last, poached and lifted the moment their shells turn red, before the smell sharpens to copper. Everything must reach the table together. You will know it is ready when the hall smells of the sea, the forest and the hearth all at once. I have cooked this twice, for the Dark Lady's officers, who said nothing and ate everything. The second time, one of them came down afterwards to tell me it was very good. He had not eaten a lobster since he was alive. He remembered what one was supposed to be." },
    [1229737] = { "Before the food, the fire. Simple wood, stacked loosely so the air can move through it, lit at the bottom and left alone. You will hear when it is ready: the crackle settles into a low hum, and the flames sink into coals. Cook on the coals, never the flames. I teach this first, before the wolf, because a cook without a fire is a guest, and I do not keep guests in my kitchen." },
    [1247741] = { "A recipe without a seller, so far, for a fish I have not seen. I am told the spinefin halibut is broad and white and armed along its back, which is how fish say they would rather not be eaten. Cook it in a thick piece, gently, until the flakes just begin to part. I write this down so that the first cook who catches one has somewhere to start. Tell me how it smelled." },
    [1249957] = { "Peacebloom steeped in spring water until the water turns the colour of straw. Do not boil it, or it smells of hay and nothing else. Steep it, and it smells of the edge of a field in the evening, behind a house I can no longer place, which is still the best smell I know. The Frostmane trolls brew it, the recipe says. I make it for the apothecaries, who need peace more than anyone and get less of it." },
    [1249958] = { "Mageroyal steeped in warm milk until the milk turns faintly violet. In Lordaeron it was served at the court, or so we said in the kitchens, the way kitchens say things about the court. I never saw a king drink it. I am not sure I ever saw a king. Warm the milk gently and never boil it, or it forms a skin. A skin on royal tea would once have cost a cook her place. Now it costs her nothing, and I still lift it off." },
    [1249959] = { "Liferoot, washed and sliced, steeped in warm melon juice until the juice goes dark and smells of earth and summer at once. Strain it carefully, because a root left too long makes the tea taste of the ground. I cannot taste it, but I can smell the ground arriving, a heavy, wet note under the sweetness. Lift the root out before it comes. The living notice. They always notice the things I cannot." },
    [1249960] = { "Sweet nectar and fadeleaf, a tea for the wounded, though fadeleaf is what rogues chew to slip out of sight. Perhaps the wounded would like to slip out of sight too. Warm the nectar until it steams, steep the fadeleaf and strain it before it turns bitter. Nobody has sold me this recipe. I have made it from a description, for the apothecaries' patients, who drank it without a word." },
    [1249961] = { "Sungrass steeped in moonberry juice until the drink turns gold. It smells of hay in the afternoon and of a warm wall in the sun. There is no sun in the Undercity. I make this tea when my students have gone quiet and pale and begun to forget what the surface is like, and I put it in their hands, and it reminds them. That is not in the recipe. It ought to be." },
    [1249962] = { "Mountain silversage in morning glory dew, two things that are hard to find, steeped together into a tea that smells of cold air and stone. Warm the dew gently, steep the silversage until the drink turns pale, and strain it. The living say it tastes of nothing much, which the apothecaries believe sharpens the mind. I believe it gives them an excuse to stop working for the length of a cup, and I do not argue with either belief." },
    [1249963] = { "Spring water, mild spice and a small venom sac, crushed and strained so that only the sharpness remains. The apothecaries checked my first batch. They pronounced it safe for the living with a disappointment I chose not to remark on. Shake it hard and serve it cold. The living say it bites the tongue. I take their word for it, and I watch them reach for a second glass." },
    [1249964] = { "Ice cold milk, mild spice and spider ichor, shaken until the ichor gives up and mixes in. It smells faintly of almonds and strongly of spider. Serve it at once. If it stands, it separates, and a separated slimy smoothie is a thing nobody should have to see. The apothecaries drink these by the jug while they work, which tells you something about the apothecaries, and I will not say what." },
    [1249965] = { "I will not attempt the name. Melon juice, hot spice and a murloc fin, shaken until the fin gives its body to the juice. It smells of a melon that has been to the seaside and come back changed. The murlocs of Silverpine would be honoured, I think, or furious. With murlocs it is hard to tell, and the noise is the same. I serve it to the brave, and to the very bored." },
    [1249966] = { "Sweet nectar, hot spice and a large fang ground to powder. The fang makes the drink chalky and thick, like milk left in the sun. Strain it well, twice, because grit in a drink is the one thing nobody forgives. I make it in the evening and leave it overnight, which is the only way the last of the chalk sinks. Soldiers drink it in the mornings. The deathguards, who fight and do not live, drink it out of solidarity." },
    [1249967] = { "Moonberry juice, soothing spice and a small flame sac, pierced and squeezed into the glass. The flame sac does the heating and the spice softens the blow. Shake it and drink it fast. I cannot tell you how hot it is. I can tell you that I once watched an orc drink one and then stand very still for a long time, looking at the ceiling, as if it had insulted him." },
    [1249968] = { "Morning glory dew, a great deal of soothing spice and a wicked claw, ground fine. The claw gives it a dark, bitter edge the spice cannot quite smooth away. It smells of crushed flowers and of something that would like to bite you. Serve it cold. The young deathguards drink it on a dare. The old ones drink it because they have stopped taking dares, and like it anyway." },
    [1250142] = { "The living like food they can eat with one hand, and this is it: stag chopped fine with hot spice, pressed once into small rounds and fried quickly. Press them twice and the juice runs into the pan instead of the guest. I have watched dockworkers at the zeppelin tower finish a dozen of these between two arrivals and go back to work without a word. That is the review that matters." },
    [1250143] = { "My students who are proud of their knife get this one: buzzard wings, boned and beaten flat, rolled around soothing spice and tied. It is slow, fiddly work, and I check every roll before it goes in the oven. Roast it gently and slice it in rounds. The spiral inside tells me everything about the cook who rolled it. A tight spiral, a careful hand. A loose one, someone who was thinking about lunch." },
    [1250144] = { "Poor farmers made this stew, chopped meat cooked long and slow. In Lordaeron it was beef, when there was beef, or I believe it was. Down here it is bat. Chop the wings fine, add the soothing spice, and give it a low fire for an evening, until the sinew softens and the kitchen smells of cloves and of something like home. My students call it bat hash. I call it supper." },
    [1250145] = { "I stew this turtle and never strain it, because it is a farm stew and not a lord's bisque. We made it by a river in Lordaeron, I am nearly sure, though I could not tell you which. Turtle and plenty of soothing spice, simmered until the meat comes apart. Leave it thick. The texture is the point, and the smell, round and warm and slow, is the reason people stay at the counter after they have finished. I have stopped asking them to move. They will, when the bowl is empty, and not before." },
    [1250146] = { "I looked for the pepper in this recipe and found none, whatever the name says, only red wolf flank and soothing spice, which does pepper's work well enough. Sear the flank hard, rest it while the pan cools, then pour its juices back over it. The smell when the juices meet the hot meat again is the whole point of the dish. I serve it to the apothecaries when I want something from them, and it works." },
    [1250147] = { "My students remember this one by its smell: big bear meat in thin slices, layered with soothing spice and roasted slowly until the layers press together into one. Slice the meat cold, or it tears. The smell when it is done is the best part, dark and sweet and close to burnt sugar. The name means nothing to me, nor to anyone I have asked. Whoever named it was showing off. Whoever cooked it first was not." },
    [1250148] = { "I rub the stag deep with soothing spice, sear it on a very hot iron and serve it at once, while it still steams. The steam carries the smell, and the smell is half the meal for anyone who can taste and all of it for me. Do not let it wait. A steak that stops steaming has started to cool, and a cold steak is a promise someone broke on the way from the kitchen." },
    [1250149] = { "Give the tiger its soothing spice, the hottest fire you can make and the shortest time you can bear. The name is a hunter's. The method is a cook's. Strike fast, then rest long, and the resting is where everyone fails, because the smell is wonderful and the fork is right there. I make my students sit on their hands. The first time, I mean it. I learned the method from a hunter who could not wait either." },
    [1250150] = { "I have read this recipe but never been sold it, which makes me suspicious of it. The ribs cook low until the meat lets go of the bone, then over a fierce flame to set a thick crust of hot spice. The smoke alone made my eyes water, and nobody I fed them to asked for more spice. The crust should crack like burnt sugar under the knife. If it bends, the ribs go back over the flame." },
    [1250151] = { "My students learn to keep a fire low on this, the toughest cut of the toughest animal. Rub the brisket with hot spice and let it cook through a whole day, until it gives under a fork like wet bread. You cannot rush it. You can only watch it, and smell it change from raw iron to smoke to something rich and dark. When it smells like a winter evening, it is ready." },
    [1250152] = { "The deathguards love this one because it keeps. The raptor cooks for most of a day over the lowest fire I can keep, with plenty of soothing spice, until it falls apart at the touch of a fork. Pull it with two forks, never a knife. It can be eaten from a bowl while walking, which suits them. Prehistoric, it calls itself. So are some of my students' manners." },
    [1250153] = { "I roll thin slices of raptor around hot spice and tie them with string. The rolls cook from the outside in, so keep the outer turn thin. Brown them on every side, then lower the fire and leave them. When the string darkens and smells of burnt hay, cut it away before anyone sees it. A guest will forgive a great deal, but not string. I learned that from a guest." },
    [1250154] = { "Spiced before and after, because raptor is lean and the first coat cooks away. I learned to smell the moment between the two. When the slices stop hissing and begin to tick, like a pan cooling, take them off and season them again while they are hot enough to drink it. My students always forget the second coat. The dish tells on them. It smells of half a recipe." },
    [1250155] = { "Giant eggs and hot spice, stirred over a low heat until they set in soft curds. It takes a long time and a steady hand, and it smells, all the while, of something rich and warm, though there is nothing in the pan but egg and spice. When the smell sharpens, stop. The eggs are about to go too far. I feed these to the deathguards after the night watch, and they eat them standing at the counter, still in their armour." },
    [1250156] = { "A raptor's egg is the size of my two fists and smells, when cracked, faintly of the swamp its mother walked through. The spice is there to answer that smell, and it takes a lot of answering. Beat the egg, season it, and cook it in small rounds on a hot stone. The rounds puff when they are done. My students eat them before I can count them, which tells me the recipe works." },
    [1250157] = { "An egg and a great deal of mild spice, more than seems sensible, from a recipe found in a condor's nest in Redridge. I have made it as written. It smells of morning and of a spice market, and of a kitchen with a stone floor that I can almost place. Beat the egg until it is one colour, pour it into a hot pan and draw the edges in as they set. Fold it soft. The living who eat it say it wakes them up. The deathguards eat it to remind themselves what mornings were." },
    [1250158] = { "There is no noodle in it, whatever the name promises. There is zesty clam meat, sliced into long ribbons and tossed in soothing spice. Cook it in a hot pan for a moment and serve it at once, tangled. It eats like noodles and smells like the sea. I do not know who invented it. I suspect a cook who had no flour and plenty of clams, and did not complain." },
    [1252566] = { "A small, bright fish with a ridiculous name. The recipe turns up in Redridge in the packs of creatures that do not cook, which means someone who did cook lost it there. A pinch of mild spice, a hot pan, a moment. The smell is sweet and quick, like fruit. I cook it when the zeppelin crews bring their children down, because the name makes them laugh, and laughter is not common enough down here." },
    [1255241] = { "Felbloom, cleansed by the druids so that it no longer does what felbloom does. I keep my distance from it anyway, out of habit. Stag meat and a giant egg give the stew its strength, the egg beaten in at the end so the broth turns silky. It smells of green things and warm broth and, faintly, of something burnt that is no longer there. I cook it for those who come back from Felwood looking as if the forest came with them." },
    [1262978] = { "Cookie is the murloc who cooks for the Defias in the Deadmines, and whoever took this recipe from him did not ask nicely. Two portions of mystery meat, one of heavy kodo and soothing spice, slow roasted together until the kodo softens and the mystery deepens. I can smell what the mystery meat was. In Cookie's kitchen, I would rather not have known. A feast is for sharing, so lay it on one great platter and let everyone reach. My deathguards do this in silence, carefully, each one leaving a little for the next, which is more than the Defias ever did. The smell fills the hall and draws the living down from the city above, and I have stopped turning them away. A murloc criminal's feast, in a dead woman's kitchen, eaten by whoever is hungry. I think Cookie would approve. I think he would want his ladle back." },
    [1263067] = { "Flour, spring water and soothing spice: that is the whole recipe for an iron oven. I believe the dough is worked into the new oven and baked hard, to season it, as a new pan is seasoned. I have never seen it for sale. A goblin trader told me of it, thinking I would be impressed. I was. I did not tell him. Goblins are unbearable when impressed upon, and they put it on the bill." },
    [1270632] = { "Flour and nothing else, baked into small biscuits for the creatures that follow adventurers about. I make them for the rats of the Undercity who have earned my respect, which is three of them. Work the flour lightly and bake the biscuits pale. They smell of warm bread and nothing more, which is just as well, because a pet that learns to like spice will never leave you alone." },
    [1270633] = { "Eggs and a little flour, the whites beaten until they stand, folded in, and baked without once opening the door. You cannot see a souffle rise from outside the oven, but you can smell it: the smell changes from wet egg to warm bread at the moment it peaks. Take it out then, and carry it fast. A souffle is a held breath. It will not wait for the guest to finish talking." },
    [1270635] = { "Crab claws, cracked and picked, bound with the least flour that will hold them, and fried in small bites. The flour is a rope, not a meal. Use too much and the bites smell of bread and nothing else. Use just enough and they smell of the sea and the pan. Crab season, the recipe calls it. In my experience every season is crab season, if you live near enough to the water." },
    [1283400] = { "Star wood burns hotter and cleaner than common wood, with a smell like resin and frost. The recipe came out of the Wailing Caverns, where the druids dreamed wrong, and someone decided a better campfire was worth the trip. Stack it the same way and let it burn down further before you cook, because a hotter fire forgives less. When the coals glow white at the edges, you are ready." },
    [1291341] = { "Thick logs, slow to catch and slow to die, a fire for the whole of an evening. Lay them in a square with a gap for the air and light them from beneath. Then wait. The smell changes when it is ready, from green smoke to dry, from sharp to sweet. That is an expert's fire, and an expert's lesson: the best heat is the one you waited for. I have not been sold this recipe. I build it from what I was told, and I was told well." },
    [1294014] = { "Berries from Hyjal, cooked down slowly with soothing spice until a drop on a cold plate wrinkles when you push it. The smell is the best part, and I can tell you that with some authority: dark fruit and warm spice, so sweet it is almost loud. The bears raid the bushes for these berries, the recipe says. I would too, if I were a bear." },
    [1295805] = { "A stew of the shore: crab, murloc eyes and hot spice. The eyes thicken the broth, so do not leave them out to spare anyone's feelings. The crab goes in at the end, so it stays sweet. The smell is the sea before anyone has tidied it, salt and weed and something still alive. My students hold their noses the first time. The second time they ask for a bowl." },
    [1295834] = { "Boar, roasted on the bone with a little mild spice and nothing else, the way farm kitchens have always done it. The recipe comes from the Riverglades, from someone who clearly had hungry people waiting. Roast it until the fat crackles and the smell turns from meat to hazelnut. Do not decorate it. A plain dish is a promise that the meat is good, and a cook who adds things is admitting the meat was not." },
    [1319313] = { "A fish in armour. The plates come off with a heavy knife and a good deal of patience, and the flesh beneath is firm and pale, like a lobster's. Soothing spice, a moderate heat, and no hurry. Armoured things take their time. The deathguards in their plate would say the same. Serve it in its own plates, washed. It makes people smile. My students learn more about patience from this fish than they ever learn from me." },
  },
}
-- (the places Classic Era's client names otherwise, swapped in there)
ns.classicNames = {  }
